-- title:
--   Lemma 11, pp.36–37 — the prepared state: $E_{x,a}=p_x(a)(I+n_{x,a}\cdot\sigma)$, $\zeta_t=\nu(-n_{x,a})$
-- statement:
--   Consider the receiver of the §8.1 construction built from a certificate: it prepares $\psi^-_{AQ}$, runs Alice's honest circuit on $A$ and measures every register other than $Q$, giving the complete record $a$. Let $E_{x,a}$ be the effect on $A$ of the complete outcome $a$ on question $x$ and $p_x(a)=\frac12\operatorname{Tr}E_{x,a}$. Then:
--
--   1. for every nonzero effect there is $n_{x,a}\in S^2$ with
--   $$E_{x,a}=p_x(a)\,(I+n_{x,a}\cdot\sigma),\qquad \sum_ap_x(a)\,n_{x,a}=0; \tag{26}$$
--   2. for every transcript $t=(x,a)$ of positive probability, the prepared state has Bloch vector $r(t)=-n_{x,a}$ and
--   $$\zeta_t=\nu(r(t))=\nu(-n_{x,a}); \tag{27}$$
--   3. conditioned on $T=t$, the sender's private state and the prepared state are product: $\omega_{SQ\mid T=t}=\omega_{S|x}\otimes\nu(-n_{x,a})$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: QPT algorithms and polynomial-size quantum circuit families with classical advice, no gate set fixed (§4, pp.17–18) → uniform (polynomial-time generated) or polynomial-size families of layered Clifford+T circuits with a fixed classical/quantum interface (ICirc); advice hard-wired; reason: Clifford+T is universal and the paper fixes no gate set (Reading 2); a non-uniform family subsumes polynomial-length advice
--   - encoding: Alice's algorithm A(x, ω_A) acts on her qubit → hypothesis 1 ≤ (c.A κ).nq: the honest Alice circuit reads at least one quantum-input wire; reason: in the circuit model a circuit may ignore its quantum input; the paper's A(x, ω_A) and the §8.1 receiver run Alice's circuit on her qubit
--   - encoding: n_{x,a} defined for nonzero effects; questions x generated by Q → one function n of (x, record), arbitrary on zero effects; (26) for every string x; reason: zero effects have weight p = 0; every x gives a POVM
--   - encoding: expectations over X and a ← p_X, conditional quantities w_{x,a}, ω_{S|x}, r(t) → sums over the question outcomes with the conditioning multiplied out (unnormalized branches), normalized only at outcomes of positive probability; reason: avoids junk values at probability-0 outcomes; the same numbers
--   - generalization: the §8.1 construction from the certificate of Theorem 6 (uniform QPT algorithms certifying ρ(v) with gap (25)), p.35 → the §8.1 receiver built from any Certificate whose honest Alice reads her qubit (no uniformity or gap hypothesis); reason: the proofs use only the circuit structure (Lemma 1, the singlet identity, 0 ⪯ F ⪯ I), not the gap or uniformity; the certificate of Theorem 6 is an instance
-- source:
--   Głuch, Morimae, From Computational Nonlocality to Public-Key Encryption and Commitments, manuscript (STOC 2027 submission, sha256 e245edd7…), Lemma 11, pp.36–37, Eqs. (26)–(27)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CompNonlocal_Certificate_recEffect
import Definitions.Def_CompNonlocal_Certificate_prepProb
import Definitions.Def_CompNonlocal_Certificate_prepJoint
import Definitions.Def_CompNonlocal_bloch
import Definitions.Def_CompNonlocal_blochVec

set_option autoImplicit false

open scoped Kronecker

namespace CompNonlocal

open QAlgorithms.Complexity

/-- **Lemma 11 (Prepared state ζ).** Głuch, Morimae, *From Computational Nonlocality to Public-Key
Encryption and Commitments*, manuscript (STOC 2027 submission, sha256 e245edd7…), pp.36–37: "Let
E_{x,a} be the effect on Alice's input qubit A corresponding to the complete outcome a on question x,
and set p_x(a) := (1/2) Tr(E_{x,a}). For every nonzero effect, there exists n_{x,a} ∈ S² such that
E_{x,a} = p_x(a)(I + n_{x,a} · σ), Σ_a p_x(a)n_{x,a} = 0. (26) For every transcript t = (x, a) of
positive probability, the prepared state has Bloch vector r(t) = −n_{x,a} ∈ S², and hence
ζ_t = ν(r(t)) = ν(−n_{x,a}). (27) Moreover, the sender's private state and the prepared state are
product conditioned on T = t."

**Formalization Note** About the receiver of the §8.1 construction (p.35) built from a certificate
`c` at `κ`: the complete outcome is the record `z` of every wire of Alice's honest circuit, `E_{x,z}`
is `c.recEffect κ x z`, `ζ_t` the normalized `c.prepared κ x z`, `T = (x, z)` has probability
`c.prepProb κ x z`. The construction runs Alice's circuit on her qubit, so it is assumed to read at
least one quantum-input wire. `n` is a function of `(x, z)`; its values on zero effects are
irrelevant (weight `p = 0`). The product clause: the normalized joint state of `S` and `Q` given `T = t`
is `ω_{S|x} ⊗ ν(−n_{x,z})`. -/
theorem lemma_11_prepared_state (c : Certificate) (κ : ℕ) (hA : 1 ≤ (c.A κ).nq) :
    ∃ n : Str → Bits (c.A κ).width → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 3),
      (∀ x z, c.recEffect κ x z ≠ 0 →
          ‖n x z‖ = 1 ∧
            c.recEffect κ x z =
              (((c.recEffect κ x z).trace.re / 2 : ℝ) : ℂ) •
                (1 + ∑ j : Fin 3, ((n x z j : ℝ) : ℂ) • pauli j)) ∧
      (∀ x, ∑ z, ((c.recEffect κ x z).trace.re / 2) • n x z = 0) ∧
      (∀ x z, 0 < c.prepProb κ x z →
          ((c.prepared κ x z).trace.re)⁻¹ • blochVec (c.prepared κ x z) = -n x z ∧
            ((((c.prepared κ x z).trace.re)⁻¹ : ℝ) : ℂ) • c.prepared κ x z = bloch (-n x z) ∧
            (((c.prepProb κ x z)⁻¹ : ℝ) : ℂ) • c.prepJoint κ x z =
              ((((c.questionProb κ x)⁻¹ : ℝ) : ℂ) • c.senderState κ x) ⊗ₖ bloch (-n x z)) := by sorry

end CompNonlocal
